                      },
                      "rawText": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Round-2 candidate: replaced the 5-kernel in-place chain of candidate-01 with a 3-kernel broadcast-fused form. run(inputs) now computes relu(1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125) via two addcmul kernels (bias and scale factors folded into cached 0-dim constant operands, second one in-place on our own output buffer) plus one in-place relu; no clone, no per-call scalar-tensor creation, no input mutation. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline. Real workspace diff in run.py only; verified by reading back the written file, not by running tests (local execution is forbidden).\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"candidate-01 (11.264us vs 15.36us baseline) removed the clone but still issued 5 device kernels for an 8192-element tensor, so the measured latency is dominated by kernel-launch/dispatch overhead rather than memory traffic. The remaining headroom is kernel count, not bytes moved.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"kernel launch count: baseline 6 kernels (mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone); candidate-01 still 5\",\"one elementwise op per arithmetic term instead of fusing the two scaled adds\",\"per-call construction of Python scalars/dispatch overhead on a launch-bound workload\"]},\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-01\",\"title\":\"Fold bias+scales into two broadcast addcmul kernels and one in-place relu\",\"hypothesis\":\"For a 32x256 fp32 tensor the p50 is launch-bound (~2.2us per kernel implied by candidate-01: 5 kernels -> 11.264us). Folding '-0.125' and the '1.25' scale into a single addcmul against cached 0-dim constants, folding '+0.75*y' into a second (in-place) addcmul, and doing the relu in place reduces the device work from 5 kernels to 3 while keeping one pass of traffic per tensor, so p50 should drop materially versus candidate-01 without changing numerical semantics.\",\"change\":\"run.py: run(inputs) now does `out = torch.addcmul(bias, x, c125); out.addcmul_(y, c075); return torch.relu_(out)` where (bias=-0.125, c125=1.25, c075=0.75) are 0-dim tensors built once per (device, dtype) in a module-level `_CONST_CACHE` and broadcast by TensorIterator inside the same kernel. The first addcmul allocates our own output (never aliases x/y), so the in-place addcmul_/relu_ mutate only that buffer; inputs are left untouched and repeated calls are pure. All other functions unchanged.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[\"baseline run.py (embedded in mission prompt): reference(inputs) = torch.relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125)\",\"iteration context candidate-01: in-place chain, 11.264us measured p50, gate performance.target failed\"],\"risks\":[\"Broadcasting a 0-dim operand is handled inside one TensorIterator kernel, but it can vectorize slightly worse than a same-shape operand; negligible at 8192 elements versus launch overhead, and full-shape cached constants could be a follow-up if it shows up.\",\"If the benchmark harness calls run() under torch.no_grad/autograd tracking, in-place ops on our own non-leaf output remain valid; no input tensor is written.\",\"Expected gain is bounded by launch overhead: even a 3-kernel form cannot approach the literal 99.9999% target (which would require ~1.5e-5 us), so this is an incremental, evidence-checked latency reduction, not a gate-passing claim.\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-01\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-01 on the shared-GPU runner and compare p50 against candidate-01 (11.264us) and naive_v0 baseline (15.36us)\",\"reason\":\"The candidate is written into the workspace run.py with a bounded, semantics-preserving diff; correctness and latency can only be established by the system's serialized GPU queue.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness on primary and small profiles; p50_us for candidate-01 with a delta reported against 11.264us / 15.36us\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"This turn made no local measurement and installed nothing; all numbers cited are from the prompt's prior evidence.\",\"The literal 99.9999% improvement target is unreachable by any real kernel (baseline 15.36us would have to drop below 2e-5 us), so the Accept Gate may still fail on performance.target even with a genuine speedup.\"]}"
                    },
