                  "id": "candidate-02",
                  "version": "agent.2",
                  "label": "Fold bias+scales into two broadcast addcmul kernels and one in-place relu",
                  "title": "Fold bias+scales into two broadcast addcmul kernels and one in-place relu",
                  "hypothesis": "For a 32x256 fp32 tensor the p50 is launch-bound (~2.2us per kernel implied by candidate-01: 5 kernels -> 11.264us). Folding '-0.125' and the '1.25' scale into a single addcmul against cached 0-dim constants, folding '+0.75*y' into a second (in-place) addcmul, and doing the relu in place reduces the device work from 5 kernels to 3 while keeping one pass of traffic per tensor, so p50 should drop materially versus candidate-01 without changing numerical semantics.",
                  "change": "run.py: run(inputs) now does `out = torch.addcmul(bias, x, c125); out.addcmul_(y, c075); return torch.relu_(out)` where (bias=-0.125, c125=1.25, c075=0.75) are 0-dim tensors built once per (device, dtype) in a module-level `_CONST_CACHE` and broadcast by TensorIterator inside the same kernel. The first addcmul allocates our own output (never aliases x/y), so the in-place addcmul_/relu_ mutate only that buffer; inputs are left untouched and repeated calls are pure. All other functions unchanged.",
